1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,768
This is STAT 331, video 11. The topic is the matrix tools that we need for multiple linear regression.

2
00:00:09,218 --> 00:00:15,682
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:16,132 --> 00:00:24,943
Type A: write a regression model in matrix form, with the size of each part. Tutorial 2, question 3 b, asks this.

4
00:00:25,393 --> 00:00:38,278
Type B: compute by hand. Products, X transpose X, the inverse of a 2 by 2 matrix, and a trace. Tutorial 2 asks this in question 3 c and in question 2 b.

5
00:00:38,728 --> 00:00:45,469
Type C: prove a matrix rule. Lecture 5 lists the rules for transpose, inverse and trace.

6
00:00:45,919 --> 00:00:55,157
Type D: take the gradient of a linear form and of a quadratic form. Lecture 6 uses this to find the least squares estimate.

7
00:00:55,607 --> 00:01:20,119
Section 1. We write the regression model in matrix form. First, a short recap. The simple linear regression model says y i equals beta zero plus beta one x i, plus an error epsilon i. The errors are I I D normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared. The gray dashed line is the true line. We do not know it.

8
00:01:20,569 --> 00:01:38,083
The second form says the same thing. Each y i is normal, with mean beta zero plus beta one x i, and variance sigma squared. The y i are independent. Each orange curve is the distribution of y at one value of x.

9
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:44,784
The data are one draw from these distributions. Each point is the line plus its own error.

10
00:01:45,234 --> 00:01:53,426
The last two lectures used the fitted line for two jobs. Here is the brainhead data again, with the fitted line in green.

11
00:01:53,876 --> 00:02:16,511
Job one: estimate the mean response mu zero at x zero. The confidence interval is mu zero hat, plus or minus t, times sigma hat, times the square root of 1 over n plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. The green band shows these intervals for all x. It is narrow.

12
00:02:16,961 --> 00:02:30,529
Job two: predict one new response. The prediction interval has an extra 1 inside the square root, in orange. The 1 comes from the new error epsilon new. So the purple band is much wider.

13
00:02:30,979 --> 00:02:58,947
At a head size of 4000, mu zero hat is 1378.7 grams. The 95 percent confidence interval is 1366 to 1392. The 95 percent prediction interval is 1236 to 1522. These formulas have one covariate. With more covariates, we need matrices.

14
00:02:59,397 --> 00:03:05,349
In simple linear regression, the mean of y i depends on one covariate, x i.

15
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:30,396
In multiple linear regression, the mean depends on p covariates, x i 1 up to x i p. Each row of the data table is one observation: its response y i, and its p covariate values. The model has the same two forms. Line plus I I D normal error. Or: given x i, y i is normal with that mean and variance sigma squared.

16
00:03:30,846 --> 00:03:39,550
So we have n equations, one for each row of the table. Matrix notation writes all n equations as one equation.

17
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,232
Stack the n responses into one column. This is the vector y. It has n rows and 1 column.

18
00:03:47,682 --> 00:03:57,133
Now build the matrix X, one column at a time. The first column is all ones. It multiplies the intercept beta zero.

19
00:03:57,583 --> 00:04:14,479
The next column holds covariate 1 for every observation: x 1 1 to x n 1. Then covariate 2, and so on, up to covariate p. So X has n rows and p plus 1 columns. We call X the design matrix.

20
00:04:14,929 --> 00:04:21,841
The parameters go into the vector beta: beta zero up to beta p. It has p plus 1 rows.

21
00:04:22,291 --> 00:04:25,917
The errors go into the vector epsilon, with n rows.

22
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:52,117
Read row i of the equation. Row i of X times beta gives 1 times beta zero, plus x i 1 times beta one, and so on. Add epsilon i, and you get the model for y i. The sizes match: n by p plus 1, times p plus 1 by 1, gives n by 1. So y equals X beta plus epsilon holds all n equations.

23
00:04:52,567 --> 00:05:04,769
Question type A asks you to write a model in matrix form, and to give the size of each part. Method card A has five steps. The picture uses a model with two covariates.

24
00:05:05,219 --> 00:05:17,209
Step 1: write the model for one row i. Here y i equals beta zero, plus beta one x i 1, plus beta two x i 2, plus epsilon i.

25
00:05:17,659 --> 00:05:24,336
Step 2: stack the responses y 1 to y n in one column. This is y, n by 1.

26
00:05:24,786 --> 00:05:37,074
Step 3: X has one column for each beta of the model, in the same order. The column of ones belongs to beta zero. Use it only if the model has an intercept.

27
00:05:37,524 --> 00:05:44,649
Step 4: beta holds the parameters in the order of the columns of X. Epsilon is n by 1.

28
00:05:45,099 --> 00:05:56,299
Step 5: check. The sizes must match: n by 3, times 3 by 1, gives n by 1. And row i of X beta must be the mean of y i.

29
00:05:56,749 --> 00:06:10,872
Here is a check of step 5 with our own numbers. There are 4 observations and 2 covariates. So X is 4 by 3. Take beta equal to 1, 2, and minus 1. Pause the video and try it.

30
00:06:11,322 --> 00:06:24,549
Row 1 of X is 1, 2, 0. Multiply each entry by the matching entry of beta, and add. 1 times 1, plus 2 times 2, plus 0 times minus 1, is 5.

31
00:06:24,999 --> 00:06:29,607
Row 2 is 1, 3, 1. 1 plus 6 minus 1 is 6.

32
00:06:30,057 --> 00:06:35,177
Row 3 is 1, 5, 0. 1 plus 10 plus 0 is 11.

33
00:06:35,627 --> 00:06:48,021
Row 4 is 1, 4, 2. 1 plus 8 minus 2 is 7. So X beta is the column 5, 6, 11, 7. Each entry is the mean of one y i.

34
00:06:48,471 --> 00:07:09,762
Now a tutorial question of type A. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 3, has 5 regions. x i j is 1 if promotion j is used in region i, and 0 if not. y is the increase in market share. The model has no intercept. Write down X and y.

35
00:07:10,212 --> 00:07:26,809
Step 1 of card A: the model for row i. y i equals beta one x i 1 plus beta two x i 2 plus epsilon i. Region 1 uses both promotions, so x 1 1 and x 1 2 are both 1.

36
00:07:27,259 --> 00:07:33,787
Step 2: stack the responses. y is 11, 2, 8, 4, 6. It is 5 by 1.

37
00:07:34,237 --> 00:07:48,872
Step 3: the columns of X. The model has no beta zero, so X has no column of ones. Column 1 belongs to beta one: 1 if P1 is used. That gives 1, 1, 0, 1, 0.

38
00:07:49,322 --> 00:07:59,178
Column 2 belongs to beta two: 1 if P2 is used. That gives 1, 0, 1, 0, 1. So X is 5 by 2.

39
00:07:59,628 --> 00:08:05,879
Step 4: beta is beta one, beta two, 2 by 1. Epsilon is 5 by 1.

40
00:08:06,329 --> 00:08:20,686
Step 5: check. 5 by 2 times 2 by 1 gives 5 by 1. Row 1 of X beta is beta one plus beta two: the mean change with both promotions. This is the posted answer.

41
00:08:21,136 --> 00:08:33,595
Section 2. Slide 7 lists the matrix rules. Question type C asks you to prove a rule, or to use the rules to show a result. Method card C has four steps.

42
00:08:34,045 --> 00:08:38,482
Step 1: write the goal. The left side must equal the right side.

43
00:08:38,932 --> 00:08:52,372
Step 2: choose the tool. For most rules, compare entry i j of both sides. For an inverse, multiply the matrix by the candidate, and show that the product is the identity I.

44
00:08:52,822 --> 00:08:58,113
Step 3: one rule per line. Write the rule that you use beside each line.

45
00:08:58,563 --> 00:09:03,512
Step 4: stop when the line is the right side. First, the definitions.

46
00:09:03,962 --> 00:09:16,229
The first rule defines the transpose. The entry in row i, column j of C transpose is the entry in row j, column i of C. Here C is 2 by 3.

47
00:09:16,679 --> 00:09:19,985
Row 1 of C becomes column 1 of C transpose.

48
00:09:20,435 --> 00:09:25,385
Row 2 of C becomes column 2. So C transpose is 3 by 2.

49
00:09:25,835 --> 00:09:32,341
Check one entry. C 1 3 is 3. It moves to position 3 1 of C transpose.

50
00:09:32,791 --> 00:09:36,567
C 2 1 is 4. It moves to position 1 2.

51
00:09:37,017 --> 00:09:43,972
So an m by n matrix becomes an n by m matrix. A column vector becomes a row vector.

52
00:09:44,422 --> 00:09:56,177
C is symmetric if C transpose equals C. Look at the diagonal of this 3 by 3 matrix, in purple. Transpose flips the matrix across this diagonal.

53
00:09:56,627 --> 00:10:08,467
The flip moves each entry to its mirror position. The matrix stays the same only if each mirror pair is equal. Here c 1 2 equals c 2 1. Both are 1.

54
00:10:08,917 --> 00:10:12,394
c 1 3 equals c 3 1. Both are 2.

55
00:10:12,844 --> 00:10:16,577
c 2 3 equals c 3 2. Both are 0.

56
00:10:17,027 --> 00:10:25,646
So this C is symmetric. A symmetric matrix is square. In regression, X transpose X is symmetric. We prove it soon.

57
00:10:26,096 --> 00:10:41,072
The transpose of a product is the product of the transposes, in the opposite order. Step 1 of card C: the goal. First look at the sizes. A is m by n, and B is n by k. So A B is m by k.

58
00:10:41,522 --> 00:10:55,986
The transpose of A B is k by m. B transpose is k by n, and A transpose is n by m. The inner sizes match, and their product is k by m. So the sizes of the two sides agree.

59
00:10:56,436 --> 00:11:10,132
Step 2: the tool is entry i j. Pause the video and try it. Line 1: entry i j of A B transpose is entry j i of A B. This is the definition of the transpose.

60
00:11:10,582 --> 00:11:23,425
Step 3, one rule per line. Line 2: entry j i of A B is row j of A times column i of B. That is the sum over l of a j l times b l i.

61
00:11:23,875 --> 00:11:34,179
Line 3: b l i is entry i l of B transpose, and a j l is entry l j of A transpose. Write them in this order.

62
00:11:34,629 --> 00:11:48,474
Step 4. Line 4: this sum is row i of B transpose times column j of A transpose. That is entry i j of B transpose A transpose. This is the right side.

63
00:11:48,924 --> 00:12:03,239
A numeric check. A is 2 by 3, and B is 3 by 2. A B is 7, 2; 3, 1. Its transpose is 7, 3; 2, 1. B transpose times A transpose gives the same matrix.

64
00:12:03,689 --> 00:12:12,883
The result: the transpose of a product reverses the order. We use it for X transpose X, and for the sum of squares S of beta.

65
00:12:13,333 --> 00:12:27,456
The inverse of B is the matrix that gives the identity I when you multiply it by B, on either side. Here B is 2, 1; 1, 1, and B inverse is 1, minus 1; minus 1, 2.

66
00:12:27,906 --> 00:12:36,717
Check the product, one entry at a time. Row 1 of B times column 1 of B inverse is 2 minus 1, which is 1.

67
00:12:37,167 --> 00:12:41,689
Row 1 times column 2 is minus 2 plus 2, which is 0.

68
00:12:42,139 --> 00:12:46,427
Row 2 times column 1 is 1 minus 1, which is 0.

69
00:12:46,877 --> 00:12:53,043
Row 2 times column 2 is minus 1 plus 2, which is 1. So the product is I.

70
00:12:53,493 --> 00:13:02,709
Only square matrices can have an inverse, and only nonsingular ones. The two columns of this B point in two different directions.

71
00:13:03,159 --> 00:13:23,831
Now take the matrix with columns 2, 4 and 1, 2. Column 1 is two times column 2. Both columns lie on one line. This matrix is singular. It has no inverse. In regression, we need the inverse of X transpose X. So the columns of X must point in different directions.

72
00:13:24,281 --> 00:13:44,377
The inverse of a product is the product of the inverses, in the opposite order. Steps 1 and 2 of card C: the goal, and the tool. The tool is: multiply A B by the candidate, B inverse A inverse. If the product is I, the candidate is the inverse. Pause the video and try it.

73
00:13:44,827 --> 00:13:53,531
Line 1: matrix multiplication is associative, so we can move the brackets. The middle pair is B times B inverse.

74
00:13:53,981 --> 00:13:57,970
Line 2: B times B inverse is the identity I.

75
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:00,660
Line 3: A times I is A.

76
00:14:01,110 --> 00:14:05,355
Line 4: A times A inverse is I. This is the right side.

77
00:14:05,805 --> 00:14:12,739
So B inverse A inverse is the inverse of A B. As for the transpose, the order reverses.

78
00:14:13,189 --> 00:14:28,143
The inverse of a transpose is the transpose of the inverse. Steps 1 and 2 of card C: the goal, and the tool. Multiply A transpose by the candidate, A inverse transpose. Pause the video and try it.

79
00:14:28,593 --> 00:14:41,863
Line 1: use the product rule for transposes, from right to left. B transpose A transpose is A B, transposed. So the product is A inverse times A, all transposed.

80
00:14:42,313 --> 00:14:45,811
Line 2: A inverse times A is the identity.

81
00:14:46,261 --> 00:14:52,491
Line 3: the identity is symmetric, so its transpose is I. This is the right side.

82
00:14:52,941 --> 00:15:04,759
A useful result follows. If A is symmetric, A inverse is symmetric too. The transpose of A inverse is the inverse of A transpose, which is the inverse of A.

83
00:15:05,209 --> 00:15:23,684
A type C question. Pause the video and try it. Show that X transpose X and its inverse are symmetric, for every design matrix X. Step 1: the goal is that each matrix equals its transpose. Step 2: the tool is the rules that we proved.

84
00:15:24,134 --> 00:15:32,475
Line 1: use the transpose of a product, with A equal to X transpose and B equal to X. The order reverses.

85
00:15:32,925 --> 00:15:46,984
Line 2: the transpose of X transpose is X. So the transpose of X transpose X is X transpose X. It is symmetric. In the example on the right, the mirror entries are 10 and 10.

86
00:15:47,434 --> 00:15:53,941
Now the inverse. Line 3: the transpose of an inverse is the inverse of the transpose.

87
00:15:54,391 --> 00:16:09,751
Line 4: by line 2, the transpose of X transpose X is X transpose X. So the inverse is symmetric too. In the example, the mirror entries are minus 0.5 and minus 0.5.

88
00:16:10,201 --> 00:16:18,009
Both matrices are symmetric. Section 3 computes the example numbers. Lecture 6 uses this result.

89
00:16:18,459 --> 00:16:28,549
The trace of a square matrix is the sum of its diagonal entries. For this A, the diagonal is 2, 5 and 1. So the trace of A is 8.

90
00:16:28,999 --> 00:16:34,269
For B, the diagonal is 1, 0 and 3, so the trace of B is 4.

91
00:16:34,719 --> 00:16:45,876
When you add two matrices, you add the diagonal entries. The diagonal of A plus B is 3, 5 and 4. Its trace is 12, which is 8 plus 4.

92
00:16:46,326 --> 00:16:54,838
3 A has diagonal 6, 15 and 3. Its trace is 24, which is 3 times 8. So the trace is linear.

93
00:16:55,288 --> 00:17:05,293
A transpose flips the matrix across the diagonal. The diagonal entries do not move. So the trace of A transpose equals the trace of A.

94
00:17:05,743 --> 00:17:18,778
The last rule of slide 7: the trace of A B equals the trace of B A. Step 1 of card C: the goal. Here is an example first. A is 2 by 3, and B is 3 by 2.

95
00:17:19,228 --> 00:17:25,521
A B is 2 by 2: 7, 2; 3, 1. Its trace is 7 plus 1, which is 8.

96
00:17:25,971 --> 00:17:36,553
B A is 3 by 3. Its diagonal is 1, 1 and 6. Its trace is also 8. A B and B A have different sizes, but the same trace.

97
00:17:37,003 --> 00:17:49,099
Now the proof. Pause the video and try it. Step 2: the tool is the sum of the diagonal entries. Line 1: the trace of A B is the sum of its diagonal entries.

98
00:17:49,549 --> 00:18:00,685
Line 2: diagonal entry i of A B is row i of A times column i of B. In the example, row 1 of A times column 1 of B gives 7.

99
00:18:01,135 --> 00:18:04,207
Line 3: change the order of the two sums.

100
00:18:04,657 --> 00:18:18,971
Line 4: the inner sum over i is row j of B times column j of A. That is diagonal entry j of B A. In the example, it gives 1. So the double sum is the trace of B A.

101
00:18:19,421 --> 00:18:25,373
The result holds whenever both products exist. The two products can have different sizes.

102
00:18:25,823 --> 00:18:42,101
Section 3. Question type B asks you to compute with matrices by hand. Tutorial 2 asks for X transpose X and its inverse. Method card B has five steps. The picture uses the A and B of the trace slide.

103
00:18:42,551 --> 00:18:54,988
Step 1: the sizes. An m by n matrix times an n by k matrix gives an m by k matrix. Here, 2 by 3 times 3 by 2 gives 2 by 2.

104
00:18:55,438 --> 00:19:20,611
Step 2: entry i j of a product is row i of the left matrix times column j of the right matrix. Here, row 1 of A times column 1 of B is 1 plus 0 plus 6, which is 7. For X transpose X, row i of X transpose is column i of X. So entry i j is column i of X times column j of X.

105
00:19:21,061 --> 00:19:28,080
Step 3: a transpose swaps i and j. A trace adds the diagonal: 7 plus 1 is 8.

106
00:19:28,530 --> 00:19:42,055
Step 4: the inverse of a 2 by 2 matrix a, b; c, d. Find the determinant, a d minus b c. Swap a and d, change the sign of b and c, and divide by the determinant.

107
00:19:42,505 --> 00:19:50,335
Step 5: check. X transpose X must be symmetric. And the matrix times its inverse must give I.

108
00:19:50,785 --> 00:20:05,953
A type B exercise with our own numbers. A is 1, 2; 0, 1, and B is 3, 0; 1, 2. Find A B transpose, B transpose A transpose, and the two traces. Pause the video and try it.

109
00:20:06,403 --> 00:20:13,784
Step 1 of card B: the sizes. All matrices are 2 by 2, so every product is 2 by 2.

110
00:20:14,234 --> 00:20:24,517
Step 2: row 1 of A times the columns of B. 1 times 3 plus 2 times 1 is 5. 1 times 0 plus 2 times 2 is 4.

111
00:20:24,967 --> 00:20:30,919
Row 2 of A is 0, 1. It gives 1 and 2. So A B is 5, 4; 1, 2.

112
00:20:31,369 --> 00:20:36,873
Step 3: transpose. Swap the rows and the columns: 5, 1; 4, 2.

113
00:20:37,323 --> 00:20:51,317
B transpose is 3, 1; 0, 2. A transpose is 1, 0; 2, 1. Their product is 5, 1; 4, 2. Step 5: it is the same as A B transpose, as the rule says.

114
00:20:51,767 --> 00:20:57,101
B A: rows of B times columns of A. That gives 3, 6; 1, 4.

115
00:20:57,551 --> 00:21:11,247
Step 3: the traces. The trace of A B is 5 plus 2, which is 7. The trace of B A is 3 plus 4, also 7. A B and B A are different matrices, but they have the same trace.

116
00:21:11,697 --> 00:21:27,057
An exercise of type B for simple regression. x is 1, 2, 3, 4. So X has a column of ones and the column of x values. Find X transpose X and its inverse. Pause the video and try it.

117
00:21:27,507 --> 00:21:37,235
Step 1 of card B: the sizes. X transpose is 2 by 4, and X is 4 by 2. So X transpose X is 2 by 2.

118
00:21:37,685 --> 00:21:46,431
Step 2: entry 1 1 is column 1 times column 1. That is 1 plus 1 plus 1 plus 1, which is n, 4.

119
00:21:46,881 --> 00:21:55,884
Entry 1 2 is column 1 times column 2. That is the sum of the x i, 10. Entry 2 1 is the same product.

120
00:21:56,334 --> 00:22:05,870
Entry 2 2 is column 2 times column 2: the sum of x i squared, 1 plus 4 plus 9 plus 16, which is 30.

121
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:13,040
Step 5, the first check: the mirror entries are both 10. So X transpose X is symmetric.

122
00:22:13,490 --> 00:22:27,229
Step 4: the determinant is 4 times 30, minus 10 times 10, which is 20. It equals n times S x x, 4 times 5. It is not zero, so the inverse exists.

123
00:22:27,679 --> 00:22:41,823
Swap 4 and 30, change the sign of the two 10s, and divide by 20. That gives 1.5, minus 0.5; minus 0.5, 0.2. It is symmetric too.

124
00:22:42,273 --> 00:22:54,689
Step 5: check. X transpose X times the inverse gives 6 minus 5, minus 2 plus 2; 15 minus 15, minus 5 plus 6. That is the identity.

125
00:22:55,139 --> 00:23:05,549
Tutorial 2, question 3 c, continues with the X of part b. Calculate the inverse of X transpose X. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:23:05,999 --> 00:23:14,639
Step 1 of card B: X transpose is 2 by 5, and X is 5 by 2. So X transpose X is 2 by 2.

127
00:23:15,089 --> 00:23:24,988
Step 2: entry 1 1 is column 1 times column 1. The column has three ones: the regions that use P1. So it is 3.

128
00:23:25,438 --> 00:23:36,297
Entry 1 2 is column 1 times column 2. Only region 1 has a 1 in both columns. So it is 1, and entry 2 1 is also 1.

129
00:23:36,747 --> 00:23:43,253
Entry 2 2 is column 2 times column 2: three regions use P2, so it is 3.

130
00:23:43,703 --> 00:23:49,357
Step 5, first check: the mirror entries are both 1. The matrix is symmetric.

131
00:23:49,807 --> 00:23:55,567
Step 4: the determinant is 3 times 3 minus 1 times 1, which is 8.

132
00:23:56,017 --> 00:24:16,817
Swap the two 3s, change the sign of the 1s, and divide by 8. That gives 0.375, minus 0.125; minus 0.125, 0.375. This is the posted answer. Parts d and f use it in the next videos.

133
00:24:17,267 --> 00:24:37,085
Step 5: check row 1. 3 times 0.375 plus 1 times minus 0.125 is 1. 3 times minus 0.125 plus 0.375 is 0. Row 2 is the mirror image. So the product is I.

134
00:24:37,535 --> 00:24:54,794
Tutorial 2, question 2 b, has two groups. x i is 0 in group 0, with n 1 subjects, and 1 in group 1, with n 2 subjects. The posted solution needs X transpose X and its inverse. Pause the video and try it.

135
00:24:55,244 --> 00:25:07,916
First card A: the model has an intercept. So X has a column of ones, and the column of x values. The picture uses n 1 equal to 3 and n 2 equal to 4.

136
00:25:08,366 --> 00:25:12,419
Step 1 of card B: X transpose X is 2 by 2.

137
00:25:12,869 --> 00:25:22,192
Step 2: entry 1 1 is column 1 times column 1. Each row gives 1 times 1. So it is n, 7 in the picture.

138
00:25:22,642 --> 00:25:33,970
Entry 1 2 is column 1 times column 2: the sum of the x i. Only the n 2 rows of group 1 have x equal to 1. So it is n 2.

139
00:25:34,420 --> 00:25:42,868
Entry 2 2 is the sum of x i squared. 1 squared is 1 and 0 squared is 0, so it is also n 2.

140
00:25:43,318 --> 00:25:57,483
Step 4: the determinant is n times n 2 minus n 2 squared. That is n 2 times n minus n 2. And n minus n 2 is n 1. So the determinant is n 1 times n 2.

141
00:25:57,933 --> 00:26:05,848
The inverse: swap n and n 2, change the sign of the two n 2 entries, and divide by n 1 n 2.

142
00:26:06,298 --> 00:26:22,938
Step 5: check with the picture. The determinant is 7 times 4 minus 4 times 4, which is 12. And n 1 times n 2 is 3 times 4, which is also 12. The posted solution uses this inverse to get beta hat.

143
00:26:23,388 --> 00:26:48,433
Section 4: matrix calculus. z is one number that depends on k variables, y 1 to y k. Collect the variables in a vector y. The derivative of z with respect to y is the column of the k partial derivatives. It has the same size as y. On the left, z depends on two variables. Each blue curve joins points with the same z.

144
00:26:48,883 --> 00:26:55,710
Take one point, y equal to 1, 2. Here z is 2 plus 4 plus 12, which is 18.

145
00:26:56,160 --> 00:27:13,973
The partial derivatives at this point are 8 and 14. So the gradient is the vector 8, 14, the red arrow. It points in the direction in which z grows fastest. To find a minimum, we set the gradient to zero. Least squares does exactly this.

146
00:27:14,423 --> 00:27:24,471
Question type D asks for the gradient of an expression in a vector. Slide 8 gives two rules. Method card D uses them in four steps.

147
00:27:24,921 --> 00:27:37,828
Step 1: expand z into terms of two kinds: a transpose y, and y transpose A y. Name the vector a and the matrix A. A term without y has derivative 0.

148
00:27:38,278 --> 00:27:42,630
Step 2: a linear term, a transpose y, has derivative a.

149
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:53,000
Step 3: a quadratic term, y transpose A y, has derivative A y plus A transpose y. If A is symmetric, this is 2 A y.

150
00:27:53,450 --> 00:28:04,415
Step 4: add the parts. Check that the result has the size of y. You can also check one partial derivative by hand. Next, we prove the two rules.

151
00:28:04,865 --> 00:28:18,540
The first rule: z equals a transpose y. Line 1: multiply the row a transpose by the column y. You get one number: a 1 y 1, plus a 2 y 2, up to a k y k.

152
00:28:18,990 --> 00:28:29,166
Line 2: take the partial derivative with respect to y 1. Only the term a 1 y 1 contains y 1. So the derivative is a 1.

153
00:28:29,616 --> 00:28:35,355
Line 3: in the same way, the derivative with respect to any y j is a j.

154
00:28:35,805 --> 00:28:43,805
Line 4: stack the k derivatives into a column. The column is a. It is k by 1, the size of y.

155
00:28:44,255 --> 00:28:49,311
So the derivative of a transpose y is a. This is step 2 of card D.

156
00:28:49,761 --> 00:29:01,473
The second rule is for a quadratic form, z equals y transpose A y. A is a square matrix. We show it for k equal to 2. Pause the video and try it.

157
00:29:01,923 --> 00:29:12,717
Line 1: y transpose A y has one term for each entry of A: a i j times y i times y j. The grid shows the four terms.

158
00:29:13,167 --> 00:29:23,621
Line 2: the two off-diagonal terms both contain y 1 y 2. Collect them: a 1 2 plus a 2 1, times y 1 y 2.

159
00:29:24,071 --> 00:29:40,476
Line 3: take the partial derivative with respect to y 1. y 1 appears in row 1 and in column 1 of the grid. The square gives 2 a 1 1 y 1. The cross term gives a 1 2 plus a 2 1, times y 2.

160
00:29:40,926 --> 00:29:46,451
Line 4: split this into two sums. Each sum has one a 1 1 y 1.

161
00:29:46,901 --> 00:29:52,917
Line 5: the first sum is row 1 of A times y. That is entry 1 of A y.

162
00:29:53,367 --> 00:30:02,519
The second sum uses column 1 of A. Column 1 of A is row 1 of A transpose. So it is entry 1 of A transpose y.

163
00:30:02,969 --> 00:30:13,103
Line 6: the derivative with respect to y 2 works the same way. Stack the two entries. The gradient is A y plus A transpose y.

164
00:30:13,553 --> 00:30:21,169
If A is symmetric, A transpose equals A. Then the gradient is 2 A y. This is step 3 of card D.

165
00:30:21,619 --> 00:30:34,440
A worked example of type D. Pause the video and try it. z is y transpose A y, with A equal to 2, 1; 1, 3. Find the gradient at y equal to 1, 2.

166
00:30:34,890 --> 00:30:43,637
Step 1 of card D: name A. Its two off-diagonal entries are both 1, so A transpose equals A. A is symmetric.

167
00:30:44,087 --> 00:30:47,905
Step 3: for a symmetric A, the gradient is 2 A y.

168
00:30:48,355 --> 00:30:58,147
A times y: 2 times 1 plus 1 times 2 is 4. 1 times 1 plus 3 times 2 is 7. So A y is 4, 7.

169
00:30:58,597 --> 00:31:03,909
So 2 A y is 8, 14. The red arrow on the plot is this gradient.

170
00:31:04,359 --> 00:31:15,133
Step 4: check by hand. Expand y transpose A y. z is 2 y 1 squared, plus 2 y 1 y 2, plus 3 y 2 squared.

171
00:31:15,583 --> 00:31:23,071
The partial derivative with respect to y 1 is 4 y 1 plus 2 y 2. At 1, 2, that is 8.

172
00:31:23,521 --> 00:31:30,667
With respect to y 2, it is 2 y 1 plus 6 y 2, which is 14. Both entries agree.

173
00:31:31,117 --> 00:31:34,189
So the gradient at 1, 2 is 8, 14.

174
00:31:34,639 --> 00:31:45,754
Exercise 3 has two parts. Part a is a linear form. Part b is a quadratic form with a matrix that is not symmetric. Pause the video and try it.

175
00:31:46,204 --> 00:31:52,711
Part a. Step 1 of card D: z is a transpose y, with a equal to 3, minus 1, 2.

176
00:31:53,161 --> 00:31:58,579
Step 2: the gradient of a linear form is a. So it is 3, minus 1, 2.

177
00:31:59,029 --> 00:32:12,320
Part b. Step 1: name A, and compare it with A transpose. a 1 2 is 2, but a 2 1 is 0. So A is not symmetric, and we use the general rule, A y plus A transpose y.

178
00:32:12,770 --> 00:32:21,218
Step 3: A y. Row 1 of A times y is 1 plus 2, which is 3. Row 2 is 0 plus 3, which is 3.

179
00:32:21,668 --> 00:32:31,076
A transpose y. Row 1 of A transpose times y is 1 plus 0, which is 1. Row 2 is 2 plus 3, which is 5.

180
00:32:31,526 --> 00:32:37,435
Add them: 3 plus 1 is 4, and 3 plus 5 is 8. The gradient is 4, 8.

181
00:32:37,885 --> 00:32:48,125
Step 4: check by hand. Expand z. It is y 1 squared, plus 2 plus 0, times y 1 y 2, plus 3 y 2 squared.

182
00:32:48,575 --> 00:32:57,855
The partial derivatives are 2 y 1 plus 2 y 2, which is 4, and 2 y 1 plus 6 y 2, which is 8. They agree.

183
00:32:58,305 --> 00:33:15,351
The last worked problem comes from Lecture 6, slide 13. Least squares minimizes S of beta: y minus X beta, transposed, times y minus X beta. Find the gradient and set it to zero. Pause the video and try it.

184
00:33:15,801 --> 00:33:22,969
Step 1 of card D: expand S. Line 1 is the sum of the squared errors in matrix form.

185
00:33:23,419 --> 00:33:32,357
Line 2: the transpose of X beta is beta transpose X transpose. This is the rule for the transpose of a product.

186
00:33:32,807 --> 00:33:36,903
Line 3: multiply out the brackets. There are four terms.

187
00:33:37,353 --> 00:33:57,321
Line 4: beta transpose X transpose y is one number, a 1 by 1 matrix. The picture shows the sizes. A 1 by 1 matrix equals its transpose, which is y transpose X beta. So the two middle terms combine into minus 2 y transpose X beta.

188
00:33:57,771 --> 00:34:13,238
Line 5: name a and A. y transpose X beta is X transpose y, transposed, times beta. So a is X transpose y. The quadratic term has A equal to X transpose X. It is symmetric.

189
00:34:13,688 --> 00:34:33,059
Line 6: steps 2 to 4 of card D. y transpose y has no beta, so it gives 0. The linear term gives minus 2 X transpose y. The quadratic term gives 2 X transpose X beta. The result is p plus 1 by 1, the size of beta.

190
00:34:33,509 --> 00:34:43,770
Line 7: set the gradient to zero, divide by 2, and put a hat on beta. X transpose X beta hat equals X transpose y.

191
00:34:44,220 --> 00:34:58,385
If the columns of X are linearly independent, X transpose X has an inverse. Then beta hat is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y. Video 13 continues from here.

192
00:34:58,835 --> 00:35:12,083
To recap. Card A writes a model in matrix form. Write the model for row i, stack y, build X with one column for each beta, put beta in the same order, and check the sizes.

193
00:35:12,533 --> 00:35:27,552
Card C proves a rule. Write the goal, choose the tool, use one rule per line, and stop at the right side. Transpose and inverse of a product reverse the order. The trace of A B equals the trace of B A.

194
00:35:28,002 --> 00:35:37,389
Card B computes by hand. Sizes, entries as row times column, transpose and trace, the 2 by 2 inverse, and a check.

195
00:35:37,839 --> 00:35:56,676
Card D takes a gradient. The derivative of a transpose y is a. The derivative of y transpose A y is A y plus A transpose y, which is 2 A y when A is symmetric. The next video covers random vectors and the multivariate normal distribution.
